#X obj 696 8 qrcode;
#X obj 230 78 cnv 1 535 426 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 83 Generates a QR code from the given string value. On
a bang or on receiving a new symbol value \, outputs the QR code as
a flat list of 1s and 0s (a black-and-white bitmap) and \, through
the second outlet \, the size (side length) of the QR code matrix.
The encoding quality is controlled by the @quality property.;
#X text 240 184 Encoding quality (error correction level). Higher levels
make the QR code more resistant to damage \, dirt or partial obscuring
\, but also increase the size of the resulting matrix. The four levels
are:;
#X text 250 254 • 'low' — about 7% of the code can be restored. Smallest
matrix size \, best for clean digital display (screens \, printing
in ideal conditions).;
#X text 250 309 • 'medium' — about 15% of the code can be restored.
A good general-purpose choice for most everyday use cases.;
#X text 250 364 • 'quartile' — about 25% of the code can be restored.
Balanced between robustness and matrix size \, suitable for industrial
and printed labels.;
#X text 250 419 • 'high' — about 30% of the code can be restored. Maximum
robustness \, recommended for codes that may be exposed to dirt \,
scratches or partial covering (for example \, on packaging or outdoor
signage).;
